  "79663905": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79894128,
    "body": "<p>It is a really frustrating situation and here is what has worked for me:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p><code>baseForegroundColor</code> and <code>imageColorTransformer</code> works only if <code>UIButton.Configuration</code> is <code>.clearGlass</code> or <code>.prominentClearGlass</code><br />\n<a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/xFdgZBfi.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/xFdgZBfi.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\" /></a></p>\n</li>\n<li><p><em><strong>THIS WORKS FOR ALL STYLES</strong></em> as stated in <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/79768392/5923606\">mhammad's answer</a><br />\n<code>withTintColor</code> and <code>withRenderingMode(.alwaysOriginal)</code></p>\n<pre><code>config.image = UIImage(systemName: &quot;star&quot;)?.withTintColor(.magenta) .withRenderingMode(.alwaysOriginal)\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/CbF9NUsr.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/CbF9NUsr.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\" /></a></p>\n</li>\n<li><p><em><strong>THIS ALSO WORKS FOR ALL STYLES</strong></em> take a snapshot of your custom view (UIGraphicsImageRenderer) and use <code>UIBarButtonItem(image: snapshot-of-custom-view)</code> or set <code>self.image = snapshot-of-custom-view</code> property if you are using custom class with a <code>tintColor</code> for <code>UIBarButtonItem</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>final class BarButtonItem: UIBarButtonItem {\n// ...\n    init() {\n        self.image = UIGraphicsImageRenderer(size: button.intrinsicContentSize).image { [weak button] c in\n            button?.layer.render(in: c.cgContext)\n        }\n        tintColor = .systemOrange\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/cJvAzegY.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/cJvAzegY.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\" /></a></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>FULL CODE:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>//: A UIKit based Playground for presenting user interface\n\nimport UIKit\nimport PlaygroundSupport\n\n@MainActor\nfunc makeButton(_ config: UIButton.Configuration, title: String) -&gt; UIButton {\n    var config = config\n    config.title = title\n    config.contentInsets = .init(top: 8, leading: 8, bottom: 8, trailing: 8)\n    config.imagePlacement = .top\n    config.cornerStyle = .capsule\n    config.baseForegroundColor = .green\n    config.imageColorTransformer = .init({ _ in\n        UIColor.systemPink\n    })\n    config.titleTextAttributesTransformer = .init({ c in\n        var c = c\n        c.foregroundColor = .blue\n        return c\n    })\n    config.baseBackgroundColor = .systemYellow\n    config.image = .init(systemName: &quot;star&quot;, withConfiguration: UIImage.SymbolConfiguration(scale: .small))\n// Solution 2. Using image renderingMode and tint\n// config.image = .init(systemName: &quot;star&quot;, withConfiguration: UIImage.SymbolConfiguration(scale: .small))?.withTintColor(.magenta) .withRenderingMode(.alwaysOriginal)\n    let v = UIButton(configuration: config)\n    v.tintColor = .blue\n    return v\n}\n\nfinal class BarButtonItem: UIBarButtonItem {\n    private let button: UIButton!\n    \n    init(_ config: UIButton.Configuration, title: String) {\n        button = makeButton(config, title: title)\n        super.init()\n        sharesBackground = false\n\n// Solution 1 and 2. Use customView\n        customView = button\n\n// Solution 3. Use snapshot image from custom view\n        // let image = UIGraphicsImageRenderer(size: button.intrinsicContentSize).image { [weak button] c in\n        // button?.layer.render(in: c.cgContext)\n        // }\n        // tintColor = .systemOrange\n        // self.image = image\n    }\n    \n    required init?(coder: NSCoder) {\n        fatalError(&quot;init(coder:) has not been implemented&quot;)\n    }\n}\n\n@MainActor\nfunc makeBar(_ items: [UIBarButtonItem], w: CGFloat = 800) -&gt; UINavigationBar {\n    var item = UINavigationItem()\n    item.leftBarButtonItems = items\n    var bar = UINavigationBar()\n    bar.items = [item]\n    return bar\n}\n\n// Present the view controller in the Live View window\nvar stack = UIStackView()\nstack.axis = .vertical\nstack.alignment = .fill\nstack.addArrangedSubview(makeBar([\n    BarButtonItem(.plain(), title: &quot;plain&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.gray(), title: &quot;gray&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.tinted(), title: &quot;tinted&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.filled(), title: &quot;filled&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.borderless(), title: &quot;borderless&quot;),\n]))\nstack.addArrangedSubview(makeBar([\n    BarButtonItem(.bordered(), title: &quot;bordered&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.borderedTinted(), title: &quot;borderedTinted&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.borderedProminent(), title: &quot;borderedProminent&quot;),\n]))\nstack.addArrangedSubview(makeBar([\n    BarButtonItem(.clearGlass(), title: &quot;clearGlass&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.glass(), title: &quot;glass&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.prominentGlass(), title: &quot;prominentGlass&quot;),\n    BarButtonItem(.prominentClearGlass(), title: &quot;prominentClearGlass&quot;),\n]))\nvar hStack = UIStackView(frame: .init(origin: .zero, size: .init(width: 800, height: 400)))\nhStack.addArrangedSubview(stack)\nhStack.alignment = .top\nPlaygroundPage.current.liveView = hStack\n</code></pre>\n",
    "creation_date": 1771785726,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 3
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79768392,
    "body": "<p>I have found a temporary workaround until Apple fixes the issue.<br />\nFor the button, it is possible to apply a tint to the image in the following way, and the color will appear in the navigation bar as we want:</p>\n<pre><code>func createButton() -&gt; UIButton {\n        var cfg = UIButton.Configuration.plain()\n        cfg.image = UIImage(systemName: &quot;eye&quot;)!.withTintColor(.systemRed, renderingMode: .alwaysOriginal)\n        let button = UIButton(configuration: cfg)\n        \n        return button\n    }\n</code></pre>\n",
    "creation_date": 1758196661,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 2
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79771866,
    "body": "<p><code>UINavigationBar</code> 's background color change can be achieved by <code>UINavigationBarAppearance</code></p>\n<pre><code>let appearance = UINavigationBarAppearance()\nappearance.configureWithOpaqueBackground()\nappearance.backgroundColor = .black\n        \nnavigationController?.navigationBar.standardAppearance = appearance\nnavigationController?.navigationBar.scrollEdgeAppearance = appearance\n\n</code></pre>\n<p>For the <code>UIBarButtonItem</code> , Liquid glass automatically chooses the tint color depending upon the background of the navigation example:</p>\n<p>Dark Background:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/26NAF73M.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/26NAF73Mm.png\" alt=\"iOS 26 Navigation Bar Image with Dark Backround\" /></a></p>\n<p>Light Background:</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/bZcPSSuU.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/bZcPSSuUm.png\" alt=\"iOS 26 Navigation Bar Image with Light background\" /></a></p>\n<p>Additionally, if you set the value of <code>style</code> property of <code>UIBarButtonItem</code> to <code>UIBarButtonItem.Style.prominent</code> it would change the liquid glass background color like this:</p>\n<pre><code>nb1.style = .prominent\nnb2.style = .prominent\nnb3.style = .prominent\nnb4.style = .prominent\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/f5njBSs6.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/f5njBSs6m.png\" alt=\"iOS 25 Navigation Bar Image with UIBarButtonItem tint colors\" /></a></p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1758560069,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 0
   }
  ],
  "79665976": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79674554,
    "body": "<p>I tried your steps in my macbook and they worked fine without any error.</p>\n<p>Your error sounds as if <code>uv</code> was installed via <code>pip install uv</code> under Python 3.13.2.\nCould that be?</p>\n<p>This is why Astral (who programmed <code>uv</code>) warns not to install <code>uv</code> simply with <code>pip install uv</code>. Because then, the <code>uv</code> gets dependent on the particular Python version. But the point of <code>uv</code> is that it should be in the level on top of the Python versions and manage them.</p>\n<p>Your <code>uv</code> seems to be installed under Python 3.13.2, thus fixed to it - which is suboptimal.\nAnd it has performance penalties - you are not using <code>uv</code> (Rust's) actual speed - but <code>uv</code> inside <code>pip</code>.</p>\n<p>The solution in this case would be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>uninstall <code>pip uninstall uv</code></li>\n<li>globally install <code>uv</code></li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code># macos and linux\ncurl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh\n\n# windows\npowershell -ExecutionPolicy ByPass -c &quot;irm https://astral.sh/uv/install.ps1 | iex&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>or just look in <a href=\"https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/#__tabbed_1_2\" rel=\"noreferrer\">https://docs.astral.sh/uv/getting-started/installation/#__tabbed_1_2</a></p>\n<p>And with the global <code>uv</code> your steps should work without problems.</p>\n<p>--</p>\n<p>Pip install might make only if e.g. you are in a Docker container where you know, there will be only one Python version forever. But even then, I wouldn't use <code>pip install uv</code> actually.</p>\n<h3>Probably your solution</h3>\n<p>I see the problem is, that you have to pin your Python version. @IRFANAASLAM was close to the solution.</p>\n<p>First, however, you should check in your parent folder of your project, whether you accidentally ran there <code>uv init</code> in the past. Is there a <code>pyproject.toml</code> or <code>.python-version</code> file? Or a <code>.venv</code> folder? If so, delete them all! Because they can interfere with anything you do in your project folder - I've recently read.</p>\n<p>Second, in your work project folder - or your home project folder - the best is in both - ensure that you do:</p>\n<pre><code>uv python install 3.9.21\nuv python pin 3.9.21\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>uv python pin 3.9.21</code> would create a <code>.python-version</code> file in your computer.\nTransferring the <code>.python-version</code> file together with <code>pyproject.toml</code> and <code>uv.lock</code> would be sufficient to ensure the correct Python version for your project.</p>\n<p>A question would be: Is your home computer a Windows? And couldn't it be that there is a problem with Python versions at home? (Does a 3.9.21 Python exist for your OS? - If not, it could use 3.13.2 out of lack of the correct version - as the next fallback). If so, I would highly recommend you, to use WSL2 (windows subsystem for Linux), where you can install ubuntu or any other linux into your WSL virtual machine and run linux in windows - while having all the files in your windows available.</p>\n<p>Ah I see you aid at home you installed using <code>curl</code>. That means - you have a macos? Probably with ARM architecture? - But I see it can't be the problem, because I have ARM and 3.9.21 exists there.</p>\n<p>You could e.g. in your work computer do:</p>\n<pre><code>uv python pin 3.9.21\nuv lock \n</code></pre>\n<p>This updates the <code>.python-version</code> and <code>uv.lock</code> file.</p>\n<p>At home then, transfer your <code>pyproject.toml</code>, <code>uv.lock</code> and <code>.python-version</code> file to your home project folder. No <code>pyproject.toml</code> file should be present in the parent folder(s) to be sure.</p>\n<p>If you run now</p>\n<pre><code>uv sync\n</code></pre>\n<p>It should sync correctly and recreate the project's environment.</p>\n<p>You have to use the pyproject.toml from your work.\nAnd the uv.lock and .python-version file.</p>\n<p>the <code>.python-version</code> file just contains the version number. Its content in this case is simply &quot;3.9.21&quot;. So one could also just generate it manually.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1750519743,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 8
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79665988,
    "body": "<p>Ok so you are using uva and still\u00a0uv\u00a0is getting confused and trying to use your system's Python 3.13.2, even when you ask for 3.9.21. This happens because uv\u00a0needs a clear path to the specific Python version you want for your project.</p>\n<p>This is usually the simplest and best way to tell uv\u00a0exactly what to use for a project.</p>\n<p><strong>1. Go into your project folder:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; mkdir sandbox   \n&gt;&gt;&gt; cd\u00a0sandbox\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>2. Tell uv\u00a0to use Python 3.9.21 for this folder:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; uv python pin 3.9.21\n</code></pre>\n<p>If you don't have 3.9.21 installed yet via uv, it might ask you to install it.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Now, create and sync your project:</strong></p>\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; uv init --package\n&gt;&gt;&gt; uv sync\n</code></pre>\n<p>uv\u00a0will now automatically use the pinned 3.9.21.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1749918666,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 4
   }
  ],
