   {
    "answer_id": 79688974,
    "body": "<p><strong>Will answer to the best of knowledge/ability.</strong></p>\n<p>What is the current state of affairs with safe C++ making its way to the standard?</p>\n<p><strong>C++26 standard is finalized. So that is the current state of affairs.</strong></p>\n<p>Could users hope for C++26?</p>\n<p><strong>No</strong>, albeit some things are in (sort of) like <a href=\"https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/header/contracts.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">contracts</a><strong>.</strong> But I guess not what you hoped for. Sorry to disappoint you.</p>\n<p>C++29?</p>\n<p><strong>Maybe. Probably.</strong> This is very hot subject as Im sure you already know.</p>\n<p>Also, is there a list of checks/safety features that may get merged?</p>\n<p><strong>Not sure what you mean - but it could possible come in some minor experimental form within C++26 work later. That will most likely be the safety profiles if so.</strong></p>\n<p>My own opinion: this is all a big mess still atm. but betting on C++29. My gut feeling is that <em>something</em> have to be changed a little in a non-backward-compatible way language wise if it has to be almost &quot;seamless&quot; . Making it safe/memory safe within compilation units should be a first goal and achievable sort of already with current language. Overall, my gut feeling is this could require multiple versions of the standard to go through as it usually is - especially if we have to deprecate something.</p>\n<p><strong>Update:</strong></p>\n<p>There is an article <a href=\"https://www.infoq.com/news/2025/06/cpp-26-feature-complete/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">here </a>about C++26 finalization, in case you want to take a closer look at it yourself.</p>\n<p><strong>Edit:</strong></p>\n<p>Clarified &quot;My own option&quot; section.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1751551315,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 3
   }
  ],
  "79686551": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79687086,
    "body": "<p>For anyone who comes across this and is looking for a solution for an <strong>expo React Native</strong> App you need to either</p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Update your expo SDK to v53 (see here: <a href=\"https://expo.dev/changelog/sdk-53\" rel=\"noreferrer\">https://expo.dev/changelog/sdk-53</a>) or</p>\n</li>\n<li><p>(in case you are looking for a quick fix without updating expo AGAIN): set <code>expo-build-properties</code> in app.json or app.config.js (thanks to @Drazar and @HabiburRaman for pointing this out in the comments)</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h1>Use <code>expo-build-properties</code> in app.json:</h1>\n<pre class=\"lang-json prettyprint-override\"><code>// app.json\n{\n  &quot;expo&quot;: {\n    ...\n    &quot;plugins&quot;: [\n      [\n        &quot;expo-build-properties&quot;,\n        {\n          &quot;android&quot;: {\n            &quot;compileSdkVersion&quot;: 35,\n            &quot;targetSdkVersion&quot;: 35,\n            &quot;buildToolsVersion&quot;: &quot;35.0.0&quot;\n          },\n        }\n      ]\n    ]\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<hr />\n<p>Original Answer:</p>\n<h1>Creating a custom config plugin:</h1>\n<ol>\n<li><p>Create a file named <code>withTargetSdk35.js</code> (or similar) in your project root:</p>\n<pre><code>// withTargetSdk35.js\nconst { withAppBuildGradle } = require('@expo/config-plugins');\n\nmodule.exports = function withTargetSdk35(config) {\n  return withAppBuildGradle(config, (config) =&gt; {\n    if (config.modResults.language === 'groovy') {\n      config.modResults.contents = config.modResults.contents.replace(\n        /targetSdkVersion\\s*\\d+/,\n        'targetSdkVersion 35'\n      );\n    } else {\n      throw new Error('Cannot modify build.gradle: unexpected language');\n    }\n    return config;\n  });\n};\n\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>Register the Plugin in <code>app.json</code> or <code>app.config.js</code></p>\n<ol>\n<li><p>If you're using <code>app.json</code> :</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-json prettyprint-override\"><code>{\n  &quot;expo&quot;: {\n    ...\n    &quot;plugins&quot;: [&quot;./withTargetSdk35&quot;]\n  }\n}\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n<li><p>If you're using <code>app.config.js</code></p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>import withTargetSdk35 from './withTargetSdk35';\n\nexport default {\n  expo: {\n    ...\n    plugins: [withTargetSdk35],\n  },\n};\n</code></pre>\n</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n",
    "creation_date": 1751444420,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 18
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79686939,
    "body": "<p>To resolve this issue, I updated my app's <code>build.gradle</code> file to target the required API level:</p>\n<pre><code>android {\n    compileSdkVersion 35\n    defaultConfig {\n        targetSdkVersion 35\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>But you still got the warning then please remove the older bundles from the open close testing.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1751438751,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 3
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79692576,
    "body": "<p>I received a warning from Google Play Console that my app must target Android 15 (API Level 35) or higher. Until now, my app has been targeting SDK 34.</p>\n<p>Here is my current <em>build.gradel</em> setup:</p>\n<pre><code>My build.gradle is \n        buildToolsVersion = &quot;34.0.0&quot;\n        minSdkVersion = 23\n        compileSdkVersion = 34\n        targetSdkVersion = 34\n</code></pre>\n<p>we want to update it to :</p>\n<pre><code>buildToolsVersion = &quot;35.0.0&quot;\n        minSdkVersion = 24\n        compileSdkVersion = 35\n        targetSdkVersion = 35\n</code></pre>\n<p>To support the latest requirements and improve compatibility, we need to add a package and added the following code in my APP.tsx</p>\n<pre><code>&quot;react-native-safe-area-context&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code>import { SafeAreaProvider, SafeAreaView } from 'react-native-safe-area-context';\nfunction App() {\n  return (\n    &lt;SafeAreaProvider&gt;\n      &lt;SafeAreaView style={{ flex: 1 }}&gt;\n        \n      &lt;/SafeAreaView&gt;\n    &lt;/SafeAreaProvider&gt;\n  );\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>After these change, my app was successfully updated to target API level 35, and the warning from Google Play was resolved.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1751879297,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 2
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79691082,
    "body": "<h1>Additional for build error on ~51</h1>\n<p>I was having a build error on ~51, just like @dchhetri. What solved it for me was:</p>\n<h2>1 - first following the instructions from @Daniel, e.g.:</h2>\n<pre><code>edit &quot;expo-build-properties&quot; on app.json as he stated\nalso added withTargetSdk35\nchanged compileSdkVersion: 34\n</code></pre>\n<h2>2 - After that, I was still encountering a build error, which was resolved by adding an additional custom plugin:</h2>\n<pre><code># Create this file at the root of your project\n# metaSpace4GB.js \n\nconst { withGradleProperties } = require('expo/config-plugins');\n\nfunction setGradlePropertiesValue(config, key, value) {\n  return withGradleProperties(config, exportedConfig =&gt; {\n    const keyIdx = exportedConfig.modResults.findIndex(\n      item =&gt; item.type === 'property' &amp;&amp; item.key === key,\n    );\n    if (keyIdx &gt;= 0) {\n      exportedConfig.modResults.splice(keyIdx, 1, {\n        type: 'property',\n        key,\n        value,\n      });\n    } else {\n      exportedConfig.modResults.push({\n        type: 'property',\n        key,\n        value,\n      });\n    }\n\n    return exportedConfig;\n  });\n}\n\nmodule.exports = function withCustomPlugin(config) {\n    config = setGradlePropertiesValue(\n      config,\n      'org.gradle.jvmargs',\n      '-Xmx4096m -XX:MaxMetaspaceSize=1024m',\n    );\n    return config;\n};\n</code></pre>\n<p>Add the plugin to the app json:</p>\n<pre><code>...\n    &quot;plugins&quot;: [\n      &quot;./metaSpace4GB&quot;,\n     ...\n...\n</code></pre>\n<h2>3 - Done</h2>\n<p>And that allowed me to build, my app is already on review by google with target SDK 35</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://i.sstatic.net/0afgXvCY.png\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><img src=\"https://i.sstatic.net/0afgXvCY.png\" alt=\"enter image description here\" /></a></p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1751722921,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 1
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79692684,
    "body": "<p>To support 35 OR set the target version to 35, below are the configurations one needs to change in build.gradle<br />\nBUT that is not sufficient.</p>\n<pre><code>android {\n    compileSdkVersion 35\n    defaultConfig {\n        targetSdkVersion 35\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Additional things to make sure,<br />\n1. Support of edge-to-edge views in your app screens<br />\n<a href=\"https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/layout/edge-to-edge\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://developer.android.com/develop/ui/views/layout/edge-to-edge</a></p>\n<p>2. Behavioural changes<br />\n<a href=\"https://developer.android.com/about/versions/15/behavior-changes-15\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">https://developer.android.com/about/versions/15/behavior-changes-15</a></p>\n<p>3. Make sure all apks into your testing tracks ( over Play Console) are with target to 35.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1751883334,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 1
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79693633,
    "body": "<p>Open the app level build.gradle or build.gradle.kts file (ie, inside the app directory). And change <strong>compileSdkVersion</strong> and <strong>targetSdkVersion</strong> to <strong>35</strong> or higher.</p>\n<p>In build.gradle (Groovy):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>android {\n    compileSdkVersion 35\n    defaultConfig {\n        targetSdkVersion 35 // Change this to 35 or higher\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>In build.gradle.kts (Kotlin):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-kotlin prettyprint-override\"><code>android {\n    compileSdk = 35\n    defaultConfig {\n        targetSdk = 35\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then sync your gradle project.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1751946706,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 0
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79693650,
    "body": "<p>Let me explain you this clearly</p>\n<p>If you are using an older Android studio update it to latest or somewhere near to latest as it can only work on target SDK 35</p>\n<p>Now canges required for target SDK 35</p>\n<p>I am using android studio Lady Bug</p>\n<p>In settings.gradle</p>\n<pre><code>pluginManagement {\n    repositories {\n        google()\n        mavenCentral() // &lt;--- Ensure this is here\n        gradlePluginPortal()\n    }\n}\n\ndependencyResolutionManagement {\n    repositoriesMode.set(RepositoriesMode.FAIL_ON_PROJECT_REPOS)\n    repositories {\n        google()\n        mavenCentral() // &lt;--- Ensure this is here for your app dependencies\n        maven { url &quot;https://jitpack.io&quot; }\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>In gradlewrapper.properties</p>\n<pre><code>distributionBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME\ndistributionUrl=https\\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.10.2-bin.zip\ndistributionPath=wrapper/dists\nzipStorePath=wrapper/dists\nzipStoreBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME\n</code></pre>\n<p>In gradle.poperties</p>\n<pre><code>android.useAndroidX=true\nandroid.enableJetifier=true\nandroid.defaults.buildfeatures.buildconfig=true\nandroid.nonFinalResIds=false\n</code></pre>\n<p>In build.gradle (app)</p>\n<pre><code>android {\n    namespace your package name\n    compileSdkVersion 35\n    buildToolsVersion '35.0.0'\n    defaultConfig {\n        targetSdkVersion 35\n    }\n    compileOptions {\n        sourceCompatibility JavaVersion.VERSION_17\n        targetCompatibility JavaVersion.VERSION_17\n    }\n    kotlinOptions {\n        jvmTarget = '17'\n    }\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>in build.gradle(project)</p>\n<pre><code>dependencies {\n  classpath 'com.android.tools.build:gradle:8.8.2'\n  classpath 'org.jetbrains.kotlin:kotlin-gradle-plugin:1.8.10'\n  classpath 'com.google.gms:google-services:4.3.15'\n  classpath 'com.google.firebase:firebase-crashlytics-gradle:2.9.1'\n\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Let me know if you face any issues. Thanks</p>\n",
