  "79760145": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79760149,
    "body": "<p><code>std::set</code> bases its ordering of the contents on a template parameter. And the ordering functor doesn't test equality; it only says if one item is &quot;less than&quot; another. As such, <code>set</code> considers two objects to be equivalent if the ordering functor returns <code>false</code> for <code>comp(A, B)</code> and <code>comp(B, A)</code> (neither is &quot;less than&quot; the other).</p>\n<p>So even if <code>operator&lt;=&gt;</code> is being called, <code>set</code> doesn't know that. All it sees is the ordering; it has to manufacture equivalence.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1757439951,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 29
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79760358,
    "body": "<p><code>std::set</code> compares items using a comparator <code>comp</code> that can only say whether or not one value is less than another. In order to check whether two values are equivalent according to <code>comp</code>, at least two calls are needed, because <code>x</code> and <code>y</code> can be determined to be equivalent only if <code>comp(x, y)</code> and <code>comp(y, x)</code> are both false.</p>\n<p>By default, <code>comp</code> is an object of type <code>std::less&lt;Num&gt;</code>, which in turn performs the comparison using the <code>&lt;</code> operator. When two objects of type <code>Num</code> are compared using the less-than operator, the compiler rewrites <code>x &lt; y</code> into the form <code>(x &lt;=&gt; y) &lt; 0</code> because there is no <code>operator&lt;</code> that can perform the comparison directly. So you get two calls to <code>operator&lt;=&gt;</code>.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1757463043,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 9
   }
  ],
  "79775796": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79806744,
    "body": "<blockquote>\n<p>Open any .tsx file. Press Ctrl + Shift + p and type: TypeScript: Select TypeScript version After that, select workspace version. It will search your node_modules for your lib and select it. The error will disappear.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>- <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/users/30972532/guilherme\">Guilherme</a> on <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/questions/79775796/next-js-cannot-find-module-or-type-declarations-for-side-effect-import-of-g#comment140767662_79775796\">Sep 29 at 23:47</a></p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1762020471,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 166
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79808674,
    "body": "<p>Create a css.d.ts or global.d.ts and add this as it's content.</p>\n<p>This tells typescript that any import ending with .css is a valid module.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-js prettyprint-override\"><code>declare module &quot;*.css&quot;\n</code></pre>\n",
    "creation_date": 1762245755,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 27
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79878514,
    "body": "<p>Sometimes the problem occurs due to the <code>noUncheckedSideEffectImports</code> configuration after TypeScript 5.6. When such a configuration is on, TypeScript raises a TS2307 error which is really confusing. Change the configuration to <code>&quot;noUncheckedSideEffectImports&quot;: false</code> or simply remove it may solve the problem.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1769674787,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 5
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79867252,
    "body": "<p>Just go in <strong>root directory of the folder (package.json level)</strong> and and <strong>add global.d.ts file</strong> in the folder.</p>\n<p>And in that file write : <strong>declare module '*.css';</strong></p>\n<p>basically you do this in order to tell typescript to not type check it .<br />\nIn short you tell ts \u201cAny file ending with <code>.css</code> <strong>exists</strong><br />\nYou don\u2019t need to type-check it<br />\nJust allow the import\u201d</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1768330129,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 1
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79921167,
    "body": "<p>There appears to be <a href=\"https://github.com/vercel/next.js/discussions/84317\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">compatibility issue</a> with Next.js and Typescript v6. Run <code>npm install typescript@5.9</code> then run through the steps in <a href=\"https://stackoverflow.com/a/79806744/532742\">Guilherme's answer</a> to ensure the app is using the workspace version of typescript.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1775511251,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": -1
   }
  ],
  "79776559": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79776608,
    "body": "<p>I ran into the same issue recently on a previously stable app, and it turned out to be related to the latest <code>bcrypt</code> release. A change in behavior between <code>passlib</code> and <code>bcrypt</code> seems to have caused problems even when the input was well under the 72-byte limit.</p>\n<p>What solved it for me was pinning <code>bcrypt</code> to version <strong>4.3.0</strong>, instead of allowing the upgrade to <strong>5.0.0</strong> (released on 2025-09-25). After making that change, the errors stopped.</p>\n<p>For example, in Python you can update <code>requirements.txt</code> from:</p>\n<p><code>bcrypt</code></p>\n<p>to:</p>\n<p><code>bcrypt==4.3.0</code></p>\n<p>This ensures your environment uses the last known stable version. If you\u2019re working with another stack (React, FastAPI, etc.), the same principle applies \u2014 make sure you\u2019re not pulling in bcrypt 5.0.0 until compatibility issues are resolved.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1758960785,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 90
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79847318,
    "body": "<p>Works with hashed pwds using 4.3 and new brcypt 5.0. Use it at your own risk, but using old version, when more secure version is available is not wise. Cannot answer questions. Thank you</p>\n<pre><code># /backend/utils/password.py\n\nimport hashlib\nimport bcrypt\n\n\n_BCRYPT_ROUNDS = 12\n\n\ndef _sha256_hexdigest_bytes(password: str) -&gt; bytes:\n    return hashlib.sha256(password.encode()).hexdigest().encode()\n\n\ndef get_password_hash(password: str) -&gt; str:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Hash password using bcrypt of SHA256-hexdigest(password).\n\n    Returns a standard bcrypt hash string (e.g., &quot;$2b$12$...&quot;).\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    prehashed = _sha256_hexdigest_bytes(password)\n    salt = bcrypt.gensalt(rounds=_BCRYPT_ROUNDS)\n    hashed = bcrypt.hashpw(prehashed, salt)\n\n    return hashed.decode()\n\n\ndef verify_password(plain_password: str, hashed_password: str) -&gt; bool:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Verify password against stored hash.\n\n    Tries legacy prehashed mode first (sha256-hexdigest -&gt; bcrypt). If that fails, it\n    falls back to checking plain bcrypt (no pre-hash). Any ValueError from bcrypt\n    (e.g., due to &gt;72 byte inputs in plain mode) is treated as a failed check.\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;\n    hp_bytes = hashed_password.encode()\n\n    # 1) Preferred / legacy-compatible path: sha256-hexdigest -&gt; bcrypt\n    prehashed = _sha256_hexdigest_bytes(plain_password)\n    if bcrypt.checkpw(prehashed, hp_bytes):\n        return True\n\n    # 2) Fallback: plain -&gt; bcrypt (only if some old records used plain bcrypt)\n    try:\n\n        return bcrypt.checkpw(plain_password.encode(), hp_bytes)\n\n    except ValueError:\n        # bcrypt v5 raises if input &gt;72 bytes (instead of silently truncating)\n\n        return False\n</code></pre>\n<p>Tests:</p>\n<pre><code># /backend/tests/utils/test_password.py\n\nfrom utils.password import get_password_hash, verify_password\n\n# noinspection SpellCheckingInspection\ntest_password = &quot;securepassword&quot;\n# noinspection SpellCheckingInspection\ntest_wrong_password = &quot;wrongpassword&quot;\n\n\ndef test_verify_password():\n    hashed_password = get_password_hash(test_password)\n\n    assert (\n        verify_password(\n            test_password,\n            hashed_password,\n        )\n        is True\n    )\n    # noinspection SpellCheckingInspection\n    assert (\n        verify_password(\n            test_wrong_password,\n            hashed_password,\n        )\n        is False\n    )\n\n\ndef test_get_password_hash():\n    hashed_password = get_password_hash(test_password)\n\n    assert (\n        hashed_password != test_password\n    )  # Ensure the hash is different from the plain password\n    assert (\n        verify_password(\n            test_password,\n            hashed_password,\n        )\n        is True\n    )  # Verify the hash matches the original password\n</code></pre>\n",
    "creation_date": 1765758693,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 1
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79956939,
    "body": "<p>As FASTAPI is a modern framework it supports updated versions and modern libraries. passlib==1.7.4 and bcrypt==4.0.1 are older versions which raises ValueError: password cannot be longer than 72 bytes, truncate manually if necessary (e.g. my_password[:72]). Try to use pwdlib[argon2]. This code resolved my value error issue.</p>\n<pre><code>from pwdlib import PasswordHash\n\n#Password Hashing Setup\npwd_context = PasswordHash.recommended()\n\n#Hashed password\ndef hash_password(password: str) -&gt; str:\n    return pwd_context.hash(password)\n\n#Verify Password\ndef verify_password(plain_password, hashed_password):\n    return pwd_context.verify(plain_password, hashed_password)\n</code></pre>\n",
    "creation_date": 1781248548,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 0
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79917596,
    "body": "<pre><code># Worked with passlib==1.7.4 and bcrypt==4.0.1\n\nfrom passlib.context import CryptContext\n\nbcrypt_context = CryptContext(schemes=[&quot;bcrypt&quot;], deprecated='auto')\nhashed_password = bcrypt_context.hash(&quot;Password&quot;)\nhashed_password\n\n</code></pre>\n",
    "creation_date": 1774942289,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": -1
   }
  ],
