   {
    "answer_id": 79830879,
    "body": "<p>I see the gist of this question as paradigm shift in programming. The idea here is to use high abstraction capabilities of C++ defining complex data structures to avoid common hazards in C, while enabling modular interoperability with other languages through stable ABI of C. This paradigm can result in better maintenance, and more optimized algorithms. But the cost is a more complicated interoperability scheme. Primary approach would be to expose C++ classes to C via pimpl:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cpp prettyprint-override\"><code>class cpp_class;\nextern &quot;C&quot;{\n    struct c_class\n    { void *const x__; };\n\n    c_class init_class()\n    { return {.x__ = new cpp_class()}; };\n    void free_class(c_class c)\n    { delete (cpp_class *)c.x__; };\n};\n</code></pre>\n<p>The rest of operations need proper wrappers as well. Moreover, I didn't dig into header+TU separation details in this snippet. So, don't comment in that regard please.</p>\n<p>A more complicated design would expose class layout to C:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cpp prettyprint-override\"><code>extern &quot;C&quot;{\n    struct c_layout{\n      /*declare undocumented members*/\n    }; //! c_layout\n\n    c_layout init_class()\n    { return cpp_class().release(); };\n\n    void free_class(c_layout c)\n    { cpp_class( cpp_class::from_c\n               , c_layout)\n     .~cpp_class(); };\n\n    int access_resource_x(c_layout c){\n        cpp_class temp\n        {cpp_class::from_c, c_layout};\n        int x = temp.resource_x();\n        temp.release();\n        return x;\n    };\n};\n</code></pre>\n<p>Now every exported accessor/modifier has to play this <code>from_c</code>+<code>release</code> tango pattern. But since it's going to repeat all-over the wrapping layer, an RAII class can be defined to ease the job:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-cpp prettyprint-override\"><code>struct cpp_release{\n    cpp_class val;\n\n    cpp_release(c_layout c)\n    : val{cpp_class::from_c, c}{};\n\n    cpp_release(cpp_release&amp;&amp;)=delete;\n\n    ~cpp_release(){val.release();};\n};\n</code></pre>\n<p>Since this RAII class is private to C++ implementation, it can be templated over <code>cpp_class</code> to reduce repetitions.</p>\n<p>This pattern allows for avoiding extra allocations when interfacing C. But most C++ std containers/classes do not expose any layout release functions. So this only works with own code. <em>Hijacking</em> resources from std containers might(I am not confident) be possible through <em>injecting spying agents</em> via <strong>custom allocator</strong> classes, but I haven't explored that area.</p>\n",
