   {
    "answer_id": 79847722,
    "body": "<blockquote>\n<p>Why?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Because the standard says so :)</p>\n<p>I would say that the rationale is to avoid having to decide whether different <code>requires</code> hide or override base function.</p>\n<p>Imagine valid code:</p>\n<pre><code>template &lt;int I&gt;\nconcept isEven = ((I % 2) == 0);\n\ntemplate &lt;int I&gt;\nstruct Base {\n\n    void f() requires (isEven&lt;I&gt;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;even\\n&quot;; }\n    void f() requires (isEven&lt;I&gt; &amp;&amp; I == 0) { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;0\\n&quot;; }\n    void f() requires (I == 1) { std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;1\\n&quot;; }\n};\n</code></pre>\n<p><a href=\"https://godbolt.org/z/3xWafxqvW\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Demo</a></p>\n<p><code>Base&lt;0&gt;</code> has actually 2 overloads <code>f</code>, one more specialized than the other (subsumption).</p>\n<p>If you add <code>virtual</code>, would <code>void f() override</code> be allowed in the derived class?\nShould the override have the same <code>requires</code> clause as in the base class?</p>\n<p>Forbidding it now allows to decide later whether it's required.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1765806144,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 11
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79847727,
    "body": "<blockquote>\n<p>I don't understand the motivation behind this.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It is mentioned in <a href=\"https://wg21.cmeerw.net/cwg/issue2488\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">issue 2488</a> that this <strong>may not be implementable in some ABIs</strong>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This would indicate that a virtual function (which cannot have a trailing requires-clause, per 11.7.3 [class.virtual] paragraph 6) can be overloaded with a non-virtual member function with the same parameter type list but with a trailing requires-clause. However, this is <strong>not implementable on some ABIs</strong>, since the mangling of the two functions would be the same. For example:</p>\n<pre><code>#include &lt;type_traits&gt;\n template&lt;class T&gt;\n struct Foo {\n    virtual void fun() const {}\n    void fun() const requires std::is_object_v&lt;T&gt; {}\n };\n int main() {\n   Foo&lt;int&gt;{}.fun();\n }\n</code></pre>\n<p>Should such overloading be ill-formed or conditionally-supported, or should the current rules be kept?</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
    "creation_date": 1765806299,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 3
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79848093,
    "body": "<p>Just because a function does not participate in overload resolution (which is what <code>requires false</code> does) does not mean that function doesn't exist. For example, notice the difference between</p>\n<pre><code>struct base { void foo(); };\ntemplate&lt;typename T&gt; struct derived : base {\n  void foo() requires std::is_same_v&lt;T, int&gt;;\n};\nint main() { derived&lt;char&gt;().foo(); } // error\n</code></pre>\n<p>and</p>\n<pre><code>struct base { void foo(); };\ntemplate&lt;typename T&gt; struct derived : base { };\ntemplate&lt;&gt; struct derived&lt;int&gt; : base { void foo(); };\nint main() { derived&lt;char&gt;().foo(); } // fine\n</code></pre>\n<p>In the first, <code>derived&lt;char&gt;</code> does in fact have a member function named <code>foo</code>. You just can never call it or even form a pointer to it, since both those operations perform overload resolution. But you <em>can</em> detect it in that name lookup for a call to <code>foo</code> on a <code>derived&lt;char&gt;</code> will hit the declaration in <code>derived&lt;char&gt;</code> and stop <code>base::foo</code> from ever being considered. This is different from what happens if you arrange so that <code>derived&lt;char&gt;</code> truly does not contain a member named <code>foo</code> at all.</p>\n<p>I think the behavior you're seeing with <code>virtual</code> makes more sense with the above remark in mind. Even if you were to delete the bit of the standard that prohibits <code>virtual</code> functions from having <code>requires</code>-clauses, you still wouldn't change the fact that a failing constraint does not cause the entire declaration to act like it doesn't exist. In particular, the way <code>virtual</code> functions work (that is, the way that the function being overridden by a certain declaration is determined, and/or the way that the final overrider of a <code>virtual</code> function is determined) does <em>not</em> involve overload resolution, but rather <a href=\"https://eel.is/c++draft/class#virtual-2\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">correspondence of signatures</a>. Thus, like name resolution, <code>virtual</code> function overriding is one of those non-overload-resolving mechanisms that can detect present but non-participating overloads.</p>\n<p>If we were to imagine a C++ standard with <code>[class.virtual]/6</code> deleted, your code would <em>still</em> not function as intended. Your <code>Derived&lt;char&gt;</code> would contain a declaration of <code>f</code> that would happen not to be callable but that would at the same time be the final overrider of <code>f</code> in <code>Derived&lt;char&gt;</code>. Even without poring through the standard to determine what would happen (and why would we be guaranteed an answer to such a hypothetical anyway?), I think we can say that this won't work. (Though, actually, I think that <a href=\"https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.def.odr#8\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\"><code>[basic.def.odr]/8</code></a> is general enough that the standard can be considered to cover this hypothetical).</p>\n<p>Given the potentially confusing semantics of <code>virtual</code> functions with <code>requires</code>-clauses, it makes sense to just ban them outright.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1765841054,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 3
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79848218,
    "body": "<p>Regarding your verbose example, where you condition the entire <code>Derived&lt;T&gt;</code> class by requiring <code>T</code> to be <code>int</code> and then overriding <code>f</code>: this is clear and unambiguous.  Only <code>Derived&lt;int&gt;</code> is a valid type and you can choose to use that or any other <code>Base&lt;T&gt;</code> including <code>Base&lt;int&gt;</code>, in which case you don't get <code>Derived&lt;T&gt;</code>'s overload.</p>\n<p>For this particular case, it would likely be simpler to declare non-template <code>Derived</code> which inherits from <code>Base&lt;int&gt;</code>, however.</p>\n<p>Your original example is a very different animal.  Here, you're allowing any <code>Derived&lt;T&gt;</code> and then trying to condition <code>f</code> specifically where <code>T</code> is <code>int</code>.  But what does a requires clause on an override even mean?  Does it only override when the condition is true, and fall back to base implementation otherwise?  Does it become illegal to call it (well, that can't be the case, because it could always be called via base pointer)?  Is it legal to define multiple overrides with mutually-exclusive requirements?  What about non-exclusive requirements?</p>\n<p>The standard is silent on all these questions, because it explicitly forbids doing it in the first place.  What is currently forbidden may later be permitted, once people have figured out the answers to these and other questions.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1765862862,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 0
   }
  ],
  "79865748": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79865763,
    "body": "<p>In structs as elsewhere,</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>const int bar</code> (aka <code>int const bar</code>) means that <code>bar</code> is a constant <code>int</code>. Once initialized, the object can't be modified directly.</li>\n<li><code>int * const bar</code> means that <code>bar</code> is a constant pointer to an <code>int</code>. Once initialized, the object can't be modified directly.</li>\n<li><code>const int * bar</code> (aka <code>int const * bar</code>) means that <code>bar</code> is a pointer to a constant <code>int</code> object. <code>bar</code> isn't constant and can be modified. But the pointer can't be used to modify the <code>int</code> object to which it points.</li>\n<li><code>const int * const bar</code> (aka <code>int const * const bar</code>) means that <code>bar</code> is a constant pointer to a constant <code>int</code> object. In this situation, the pointer is constant, and it can't be used to modify the <code>int</code> object to which it points.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The <a href=\"https://c-faq.com/decl/spiral.anderson.html\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">spiral rule</a> can be used to &quot;decipher&quot; the meaning of a declaration. If there's nothing that follows the name being declared, it simplifies to reading from back to front.)</p>\n<p>This is easy to check!</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-c prettyprint-override\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstruct Foo {\n    const int bar;  // `bar` is a constant `int`\n};\n\nint main( void ) {\n   struct Foo foo = { 123 };\n   foo.bar = 456;  // error: assignment of read-only member 'bar'\n   printf( &quot;%d\\n&quot;, foo.bar );\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Changing to a pointer makes no difference:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-c prettyprint-override\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstruct Foo {\n    int * const bar;  // `bar` is a constant pointer to an `int`\n};\n\nint main( void ) {\n   int i = 123;\n   int j = 456;\n   struct Foo foo = { &amp;i };\n   foo.bar = &amp;j;        // error: assignment of read-only member 'bar'\n   *( foo.bar ) = 789;  // ok\n   printf( &quot;%d\\n&quot;, *( foo.bar ) );\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>In your second snippet, it's not <code>bar</code> that's constant but <code>*bar</code>.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-c prettyprint-override\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstruct Foo {\n    const int * bar;  // `bar` is a pointer to a constant `int`\n};\n\nint main( void ) {\n   int i = 123;\n   int j = 456;\n   struct Foo foo = { &amp;i };\n   foo.bar = &amp;j;        // ok\n   *( foo.bar ) = 789;  // error: assignment of read-only location '*foo.bar'\n   printf( &quot;%d\\n&quot;, *( foo.bar ) );\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>Finally, both the pointer and that to which it points are constant in the following snippet:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-c prettyprint-override\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nstruct Foo {\n    const int * const bar;  // `bar` is a constant pointer to a constant `int`\n};\n\nint main( void ) {\n   int i = 123;\n   int j = 456;\n   struct Foo foo = { &amp;i };\n   foo.bar = &amp;j;        // error: assignment of read-only member 'bar'\n   *( foo.bar ) = 789;  // error: assignment of read-only location '*foo.bar'\n   printf( &quot;%d\\n&quot;, *( foo.bar ) );\n}\n</code></pre>\n",
    "creation_date": 1768169086,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 32
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79865759,
    "body": "<p>In the first example, <code>bar</code> is constant and you can not change its value once initialized.</p>\n<p>In the second case <code>bar</code> itself is not constant, but the data it points to is.</p>\n<p>So in the second case you can assign to <code>bar</code> anytime you like, but in the first case it's not possible.</p>\n<p>That <code>bar</code> is a member inside a structure doesn't matter, it has the same semantics as for the same declarations anywhere else.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1768168558,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 16
   }
  ],
  "79868298": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79868374,
    "body": "<p>TLDR for those who came here looking for clues when facing Bochs' log like <code>fetch_raw_descriptor: GDT: index (ff57) 1fea &gt; limit (2f)</code>:</p>\n<p>Start Bochs with the debugger enabled, execute <code>show int</code> &amp; <code>show dbg_all</code> in the Bochs debugger CLI, and then run your image. This would make Bochs to log all interrupts/exceptions; if this error occurred during interrupt handling, you'll get some more clues into what was happening.</p>\n<hr />\n<p>Now, to the question.\nFirst let's see the logs.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>19849108391e[CPU0  ] fetch_raw_descriptor: GDT: index (ff57) 1fea &gt; limit (2f)\n19849108391e[CPU0  ] interrupt(): gate descriptor is not valid sys seg (vector=0x08)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Vector 0x08 is a double fault vector. So presumably there was some exception, and the attempt to process it resulted in a double fault. Moreover, the attempt to process a double fault\nalso resulted in a fault, presumably because the trap segments value in IDT was not a valid system segment. Something is wrong in your IDT; and also there was that first exception, so something is wrong there too.</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>19849108391i[CPU0  ] | EAX=00100023...\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] | ESP=00800000...\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] | SEG sltr(index|ti|rpl)     base    limit G D\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] |  CS:001b( 0003| 0|  3) 00000000 ffffffff 1 1\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] |  DS:0023( 0004| 0|  3) 00000000 ffffffff 1 1\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] |  SS:0023( 0004| 0|  3) 00000000 ffffffff 1 1\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] | EIP=00100023 (00100023)\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] | CR2=0x00100023\n19849108391i[CPU0  ] 0x0000000000100023&gt;&gt; or byte ptr ds:[esi-117], ah : 08668B\n</code></pre>\n<p>Ok, so the exception happens upon jump to User mode. CS is 0x1B as expected, and the rest of the segments are 0x23 as expected. ESP is 0x800000 as expected. It seems the cross-privilege level iret did its job. The segment shadow contents looks fine as well.</p>\n<p>EIP looks wrong however. It points into the kernel, somewhere in the middle of an instruction. CR2 is the same as EIP which suggests the initial exception was a paging exception; CPU couldn't read the code instruction (which is expected I guess if the kernel pages are set as system). EAX is the same as EIP (and it holds the initial EIP value in <code>enter_user_mode</code>, so probably the very first instruction faulted).</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>19849108391e[CPU0  ] exception(): 3rd (13) exception with no resolution, shutdown status is 00h, resetting\n</code></pre>\n<p>This is the triple-fault reset, because a double-fault could not be processed.</p>\n<p>Ok, so 1) exception handling doesn't work, and it should(?) and 2) a bad EIP.</p>\n<p>That 0x23 in the end of EIP is highly suspect, because it matches the data segment value. Surely enough, <code>enter_user_mode</code> is buggy:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>  00100b10 &lt;enter_user_mode&gt;:\n  100b10:       fa                      cli\n  100b11:       8b 44 24 04             mov    0x4(%esp),%eax\n  100b15:       66 b8 23 00             mov    $0x23,%ax\n</code></pre>\n<p>EIP value gets loaded into <code>eax</code>, but then <code>ax</code> is also loaded with 0x23 which destroys half of the value. Fix is trivial: simply use different registers; e.g. use 'dx' for the selector value.</p>\n<p>With that fixed, we get</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>19960859176i[CPU0  ] | EIP=00108000 (00108000)\n19960859176i[CPU0  ] | CR2=0x00108000\n19960859176i[CPU0  ] 0x0000000000108000&gt;&gt; mov eax, 0xdeadbeef : B8EFBEADDE\n</code></pre>\n<p>Ok, so now it faults at the actual usermode start. Why does it do that? Well, because it\u2019s still a system-mode page. There is <em>nothing</em> in your code to prepare the actual user-mode region of virtual addresses. From the book (para 11.2):</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>There are a few things every user mode process needs:\nPage frames for code, data and stack. At the moment it suffices to allocate one page frame for the stack and enough page frames to fit the program\u2019s code. Don\u2019t worry about setting up a stack that can be grow and shrink at this point in time, focus on getting a basic implementation work first.\nThe binary from the GRUB module has to be copied to the page frames used for the programs code.\nA page directory and page tables are needed to map the page frames described above into memory. At least two page tables are needed, because the code and data should be mapped in at 0x00000000 and increasing, and the stack should start just below the kernel, at 0xBFFFFFFB, growing towards lower addresses. The U/S flag has to be set to allow PL3 access.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>None of that has been done. There isn\u2019t any stack space allocated, and there aren\u2019t any user-mode pages to hold the user-space code. This fault is expected; the kernel just isn't ready to run userspace.</p>\n<p>Ok, with that figured out, let's return to the IDT handling. Looking at <code>interrupts_install_idt</code>, it is immediately obvious that it could use some love. At least the internal exceptions handlers should be all filled up (also note exception 7 is not error-code bearing; the list of such exceptions is provided in the book). It would be a good idea to add <em>some</em> handling of the exceptions also; one common approach is to log an exception, CPU, state, etc. Well, see the Bochs log for an example. Your code could do something similar.</p>\n<p>I have to say the book has dropped the ball here. This is a tricky subject, and the book gives two rough examples of C side (paragraph 6.3) and assembly side (paragraph 6.4) of interrupt handler, but they are not compatible with each other! <code>interrupt_handler</code> has to be called according to its declaration (or vice versa); kudos to you for fixing that (I am not sure if struct ordering is correct though).</p>\n<p>All that said, the code does look fine at first glance. It should've been a page fault, which should've been handled at least somehow. Moreover, Bochs debugger can show IDT contents, and it's fine; the segment values are correct. Let's switch Bochs' debugging logs on and see what's happening in more details (&quot;show int&quot;, &quot;show dbg_all&quot; in the Bochs debugger CLI):</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-none prettyprint-override\"><code>CPU 0: Exception 0x0e - (#PF) page fault occurred (error_code=0x0005)\nCPU 0: Interrupt 0x0e occurred (error_code=0x0005)\n07358149912e[CPU0  ] fetch_raw_descriptor: GDT: index (ff57) 1fea &gt; limit (2f)\nCPU 0: Exception 0x0a - (#TS) invalid TSS occurred (error_code=0xff51)\nCPU 0: Exception 0x08 - (#DF) double fault occurred (error_code=0x0000)\nCPU 0: Interrupt 0x08 occurred (error_code=0x0000)\n07358149912e[CPU0  ] fetch_raw_descriptor: GDT: index (ff57) 1fea &gt; limit (2f)\nCPU 0: Exception 0x0a - (#TS) invalid TSS occurred (error_code=0xff51)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Ah yes, IDT itself is not an issue. Here we have a cross-privilege trap (switching from user-mode at ring 3 to kernel at ring 0). That involves switching the stack, and switching the stack needs TSS (so the CPU has a place to store ring 3 SS/ESP and load ring 0 SS/ESP). There isn\u2019t any TSS set in the code, and therefore any exceptions in ring 3 would result in a crash. Well, the book hints at such problems in paragraph 11.3:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>For now, we should have interrupts disabled, as it requires a little more work to get inter-privilege level interrupts to work properly (see the section \u201cSystem calls\u201d).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>And indeed, later on in section &quot;system calls&quot; (paragraph 13.2), it talks about setting up TSS and why it is needed. Without that done, you have to rely on Bochs' debugger and logs to deal with any bugs.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1768468637,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 23
   }
  ],
  "79871713": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79884587,
    "body": "<p>In my case, I was getting the same warning and animation glitch when a <code>Menu</code> was inside a <code>GlassEffectContainer</code> with <code>.buttonStyle(.glass)</code> and .<code>glassEffectUnion()</code>.</p>\n<p>Adding <code>.compositingGroup()</code> before the button style fixed it:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-swift prettyprint-override\"><code>Menu {\n    // menu content...\n} label: {\n    Image(systemName: &quot;ellipsis&quot;)\n}\n.compositingGroup()     //  fixes _UIReparentingView warning\n.buttonStyle(.glass)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The <code>.compositingGroup()</code> creates a rendering boundary that isolates the <code>Menu</code> from parent effects, preventing the UIKit view hierarchy conflict when the menu presents.</p>\n<p>Even if you're not using glass effects, <code>.compositingGroup()</code> on its own might still resolve your issue since it seems to be a general SwiftUI/Menu bug in iOS 26 related to how <code>Menu</code>'s UIKit presentation interacts with the view hierarchy.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1770408909,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 23
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79958545,
    "body": "<p>I had this same issue and found that\u00a0<code>.compositingGroup()</code>\u00a0did not fix it for me (it resolved the specific error in the console but not the behavior of the <code>Menu()</code>). In my case the root cause was sheets being split across different levels of the view hierarchy (some on a parent view, others on a descendant view deep inside it). Moving all\u00a0<code>.sheet</code>\u00a0modifiers up to a single common ancestor fixed it.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1781362682,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 1
   }
  ],
  "79990062": [
   {
    "answer_id": 79990066,
    "body": "<p>For historic reasons, the C standard allows <code>NULL</code> to be either an integer constant expression with value 0 or such an expression cast to <code>void *</code>. So <code>NULL</code> could be defined as <code>0</code>, which would have type <code>int</code>. It could even be <code>0L</code> or <code>0ULL</code>, which would have type <code>long</code> or <code>unsigned long long</code>, respectively, since these are also integer constant expressions with value 0.</p>\n<p>Then the sentinel argument to your variadic function would have a type incompatible with the type of the strings you are passing. You could be passing <code>int</code> while the strings result in <code>char *</code>. When your function attempted to get the value of this sentinel argument using <code>va_arg(\u2026, char *)</code>, the behavior would not be defined.</p>\n<p>Additionally, note that your string arguments have type <code>char *</code>, not <code>const char *</code>. Again, this is for historic reasons; <code>const</code> was not originally in C, so string literals had type <code>char *</code>, and this was not changed when <code>const</code> was introduced.</p>\n<p>So, to make your sentinel argument exactly match the strings, you should pass <code>(char *) NULL</code>.</p>\n<h2>Note</h2>\n<p>You can use <code>(const char *) NULL</code> even though <code>const char *</code> is not compatible with <code>char *</code>. This is because the C standard makes some exceptions in what types may be used with <code>va_arg</code>. C 2024 7.16.2.1 says it will work if:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\u2026 both types [the actual type of the argument and the type passed to <code>va_arg</code>] are pointers to qualified or unqualified versions of compatible types;\u2026</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
    "creation_date": 1785753287,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 14
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79990065,
    "body": "<p>The problem is that <code>NULL</code> is frequently defined as 0, <code>int</code> rather than a pointer type in C++ and C, or rarely <code>(void*)0</code> in C.</p>\n<p>A variadic function with ellipses <code>...</code> does not know what type it's receiving. This is a key point of the undefined behavior - when the function extracts the argument, it expects to receive <code>char*</code> but <code>int</code> might be put.</p>\n<p>You apply a correct solution, you explicitly set the argument type compatible with the types of the previous arguments, the compiler puts a pointer, and the function properly extracts pointers until <code>NULL</code>.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1785753133,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 5
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79990438,
    "body": "<p>As noted by the other answers, NULL could be a <code>0</code> value or a <code>(void*)0</code> . The value <code>0</code> is <em>generally</em> equivalent to <code>NULL</code>, but in this case if <code>NULL</code> is defined as <code>0</code> it may the wrong result.</p>\n<p>Consider an <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/64-bit_computing#64-bit_data_models\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">LP64</a> model, where int is 32 bits and a pointer 64 bits wide. A variadic call to</p>\n<pre><code>EXAMPLE(line, 0, &quot;typedef&quot;, &quot;unsigned&quot;, &quot;int&quot;, NULL);\n</code></pre>\n<p>where the last element is a <code>0</code> integer would push a 32 bit integer into the stack, but when received as a 64-bit pointer, the other 32 bits popped will be arbitrary (whatever happened to be at that location), but probably different than zero, and thus it will be treated as a non-null pointer to a random address, a most likely end up with a <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segmentation_fault\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">segmentation fault</a>.</p>\n<p>I actually experienced this many years ago, in what I think was an <code>execl()</code> that ended with a <code>0</code> sentinel instead of <code>(char*) 0</code> or <code>NULL</code>. A <code>0</code> sentinel was supposedly acceptable according to the documentation, but it actually led to that weird segmentation fault (possibly a &quot;working program&quot; that broke when [compiling and] running on a different machine).</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1785854700,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 4
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 79990447,
    "body": "<p><code>NULL</code> is defined as a macro in several standard headers in C (<code>&lt;stddef.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;stdio.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;stdlib.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;string.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;time.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;wchar.h&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;locale.h&gt;</code>...)</p>\n<p>The C Standard specifies that it <em>expands to an implementation-defined null pointer constant</em>.  Implementations can chose to define <code>NULL</code> as <code>((void*)0)</code> or <code>0</code> or some other way as long as the exansion fits the definition of a <em>null pointer constant</em>.</p>\n<p>When <code>NULL</code> is passed to a vararg function, the compiler merely passes the expansion of the macro, whose type is not fully specified: it would be <code>int</code> if <code>NULL</code> expands to <code>0</code> and <code>void*</code> if it expands to <code>((void*)0)</code>.  The vararg function must make an assumption about the actual type of the arguments that were passed to it to retrieve their value.  This assumption will fail if <code>NULL</code> was passed to a function expecting a <code>void</code> pointer and <code>0</code> was actually passed.  This is a case of undefined behavior.</p>\n<p>Here is a simple example:</p>\n<pre class=\"lang-c prettyprint-override\"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(&quot;%p\\n&quot;, NULL);  // potential undefined behavior\n    return 0;\n}\n</code></pre>\n<p>The above code may have undefined behavior (as opposed to implementation defined behavior as expected).</p>\n<p>To avoid this pitfall, null pointers must be passed explicitly as <code>(void*)0</code> to vararg functions and the warning does help avoid potential undefined behavior.</p>\n<p>The definition of <code>NULL</code> in C is a mostly a quality of implementation issue:  defining <code>NULL</code> as <code>((void*)0</code> avoids some other pitfalls: setting a null byte with <code>*p = NULL</code> instead of <code>*p = 0</code> or better <code>*p = '\\0'</code> will be caught as an error with this definition.</p>\n<p>Note however that <code>NULL</code> cannot be defined as <code>((void*)0)</code> in C++ because, contrary to what happens in C, <code>void*</code> does not implicitly convert to other pointer types in C++. This makes this discussion even more compelling for C++.</p>\n<p>Note finally that C29 will introduce a new predefined constant <code>nullptr</code>, heavily inspired by C++, that can safely be passed to vararg functions expecting a null pointer.</p>\n",
    "creation_date": 1785856222,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 3
   }
  ],
  "80000170": [
   {
    "answer_id": 80000180,
    "body": "<p>This is explained in the <a href=\"https://www.open-std.org/jtc1/sc22/wg21/docs/papers/2024/p3309r3.html#wait-and-notify\" rel=\"noreferrer\">proposal</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Wait and notify operations should work during constant evaluation as expected in a single-threaded environment. Notify will be noop and wait-ing for a different value will be a deadlock which should result in constant evaluation failure according to [expr.const#5.7] an expression that would exceed the implementation-defined limits as every check for the value is considered [intro.progress#4] continous executing of execution steps while waiting for the condition.</p>\n</blockquote>\n",
    "creation_date": 1788350525,
    "is_accepted": false,
    "score": 21
   },
   {
    "answer_id": 80000374,
    "body": "<p>The key point is that a compile-time <code>wait()</code> doesn't actually put a compiler thread to sleep.</p>\n<p>During constant evaluation there is no other thread that can change the atomic or call <code>notify_*</code>. So if the value is already different from <code>old</code>, the wait can finish normally. If it isn't, the evaluation effectively keeps waiting.</p>\n<p>The standard deals with this by treating a blocking library call as continuously executing while it waits. Eventually the implementation's constant-evaluation limits are reached, and the expression fails to be a constant expression.</p>\n<p>So it doesn't need to guarantee that the compiler literally wakes the wait. An unmatched <code>wait()</code> simply cannot successfully complete during constant evaluation.</p>\n",
